governor tell us  why someone who is already facing huge  cost of living pressures around energy  bills now has to face increases to their  borrowing costs on top it's a very  important question it's probably the  most important question i realize the  distress because it is a very difficult  situation to both have such a large  increase in inflation  and to be forecasting that the will bomb  threat be a recession and to be saying  we have to increase and to increase uh  interest rates  and the reason is this we've had this  very big huge external shock uh you know  in the three months since we last did  the monetary policy report i mean the  change to the  you know the whole energy price  particularly gas price uh regime has  been massive and i'm afraid that has to  be put at the door of russia there's no  question about that now  very reasonable to ask the question so  why are you raising interest rates in  that context because you can see that  yes inflation's going to get very high  but you know other things equal  it ought to come down again and we do  project that actually  the worry for us is are other things  going to be equal and the the really  difficult situation this is why i'm  afraid we have to do it is  if we have more persistent inflation  that is harder for everybody we'll have  to raise rates by more  and and those you know people and  particularly those on low incomes will  be even more affected and that's the  thing that concerns me so is it the case  this has got to hurt if it's going to  work the reason we're doing it is  because if we don't get  the inflation under control if we don't  bring it down from where i'm afraid it's  got to go up to because of the say of  this huge energy  shock that we're having  if we don't bring it down  then then the damage and the distress  will be even greater  the main pressure is external  gas prices  everyone understands that  is it really the case that you need to  tighten the screw at home is that  judgment are you sure about that  judgment  you're right that the main pressure is  external and that's going to feed  through into inflation i'm afraid over  this winter we do think it's going to  come down you know going through next  year and  beyond  but we have got another thing going on  which is domestic the size of the labor  force has shrunk there are fewer people  you know working in the economy  and that's affecting uh you know when i  go around the country a lot of spend a  lot of time going around the country the  first thing that every firm wants to  talk to me about is i can't hire enough  people  and yeah we've got a situation where  we're saying well i think you know we  can already see the economy slowing it's  going to get worse i'm afraid and yet  firms are saying  two things to me one is they're still  able to increase prices and they're not  getting resistance and they just can't  hire enough people and of course that  does have an upward effect on uh  on costs and you've talked about wages  in the past somewhat controversially in  the context of these inflation numbers i  mean above 10 percent well into next  year it's really reasonable to ask  people to accept  rises you know below 5  if everybody tries to get ahead of  inflation and we get inflation more  embedded we'll get more persistent  inflation  and that's why i said particularly you  know if you seek a high if you seek to  beat inflation  it will get into an upward you know  trend and we will have to we will have  to raise interest rates even more then  and that will be more painful okay um  much noise politically we have a contest  to become prime minister and you don't  want to get directly involved but there  are  questions about changing the mandate of  the bank and  some blame around liz trust certainly  her campaign  at the bank's door for this is this the  time to be  addressing the work you do here or is  that risk making a difficult situation  even worse well i i  i'm not going to get involved in the  campaign and the leadership election to  be leader of the conservative party the  next prime minister it's what i would  emphasize is that i think central bank  independence is an absolutely key part  of the economic system this country and  in other countries  it's it's an important part and  never more than now  this is the biggest test of our system  in the quarter of a century that it's  existed for last one if i may you talked  about a judgment called walking this  walking this path  consequences on either side  of getting it wrong more have you  thought about the consequences if you  have you and your colleagues have got  this wrong  we don't look at it from the point of  saying you know personally what if we  get it wrong this is about for the  country and for the economy so we spend  a lot of time looking at those risks and  you're confident you've got it right  we think this is  undoubtedly the right thing to do but we  will be back in you know about six weeks  time because that's the next monetary  policy committee meeting and we will  have to go all through the evidence  again there'll be new evidence and we  will do that all over again

